---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";

const question = "How long does dry lining last?";
const answer = `Properly installed dry lining will last the lifetime of the building — typically 50+ years for the plasterboard itself, and indefinitely for metal stud components.

Lifespan by component:

- Standard plasterboard: 50+ years (some 19th-century lath-and-plaster ceilings are still going strong)
- Metal stud (galvanised): 100+ years — non-corrosive in dry interior conditions
- Jointing compound: 30+ years (may need re-decoration sooner)
- Insulation: 50+ years (PIR and mineral wool are stable for the lifetime of the building)

Common failure causes (all preventable):

- Water damage from leaks — address plumbing issues immediately
- Damp penetration from outside — maintain pointing, render, and DPC
- Impact damage — use Type D (impact-resistant) board in high-traffic areas
- Poor original installation — choose a contractor who works to BS 8212

For Maidstone properties, dry lining done in the 1970s-80s is still performing well where the original installation was to spec.`;
const description = `Properly installed dry lining will last the lifetime of the building — typically 50+ years for the plasterboard itself, and indefinitely for metal stud componen`;
const canonical = "/drylining-faq/how-long-does-dry-lining-last/";

